<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<meta name="description" content="Every figure on this page is traced to a page of the City of Livermore's published budget, and what we inferred is marked as inferred.">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
<script>
  // Stamp the saved theme before first paint. Inline and tiny on purpose: a
  // deferred script would repaint the page in the wrong mode first.
  try {
    var t = localStorage.getItem("fisc-theme");
    if (t === "light" || t === "dark") document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t;
  } catch (e) { /* private mode: fall back to the OS setting */ }
</script>
</head>
<body>

<div class="wip-banner" role="status">⚠️ Work in progress — early preview, coverage is incomplete.</div>

{{- if gt (len .Nav) 1}}
<!-- Every page carries the whole nav, which is what makes the set of views
     visible from any one of them. Flat .html pages rather than hash routing:
     each view renders its figures server-side, and under routing views 2..N
     would have no server-rendered content at all. -->
<nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Views">
  <ul>
    {{- range .Nav}}
    <li>{{if .Current}}<span aria-current="page">{{.Label}}</span>{{else}}<a href="{{.Path}}">{{.Label}}</a>{{end}}</li>
    {{- end}}
  </ul>
</nav>
{{- end}}

<header class="page-head">
  <div>
    <h1>Where Livermore's money comes from, and where it goes</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <!-- "all funds, gross" is a HAND-WRITTEN RENDERING of one scope slug,
           all-funds-gross, and the coupling is not visible from here. The
           footer's "Scope {{.Scope}}" prints the slug; this prints English.
           Nothing repaints either, so a view built on another scope would ship
           a lede contradicting its own footer. TestTheLedesProseNamesThePages
           Scope pins the pair. -->
      The <span id="lede-year">{{.FiscalYearLabel}} {{.Basis}}</span> budget, all funds, gross — read out of the city's
      published schedule and traced back to it. Figures the city printed are drawn solid;
      the two things we inferred are drawn dashed and labelled.
      {{- /* EMPTY UNTIL THE VIEW DECLARES STEPS. app.js's paintChartHint owns
             the words and says nothing on a page with no chain; the element
             is here so the two chart templates put the hint in the same place
             and the client reaches for one id on both. */}}
      <span id="chart-hint"></span>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="head-controls">
    {{- if gt (len .Years) 1}}
    <!-- A radio group, not a <select>: two options that swap the whole page are
         a choice between peers, and a radio group says which one you are
         looking at without being opened.

         IT SHIPS DISABLED and app.js enables it. Switching year is entirely a
         client behaviour — it refetches a document and repaints — so with
         JavaScript off the control could be moved and nothing would follow it:
         the reader would select FY2026-27 and go on reading FY2025-26's
         figures, with the styling agreeing with them. A control that lies is
         worse than one that is absent, and the year on show is still stated in
         the lede either way. -->
    <fieldset class="year-toggle" id="year-toggle" disabled>
      <legend>Budget year</legend>
      {{- /* The id is keyed on the STEM and not on the Year (fisc-rmx). A stem
             is unique by construction -- buildProjections refuses two documents
             at one -- while a year is not: two BASES for one fiscal year are two
             documents, and keying on the year gave both radios the same id, so
             every <label for=...> pointed at whichever parsed first and clicking
             one year's label selected the other. */}}
      {{- range $i, $y := .Years}}
      <input type="radio" name="fisc-year" id="year-{{$y.Stem}}" value="{{$y.Stem}}"
             {{if eq $i 0}}checked{{end}}>
      <label for="year-{{$y.Stem}}">{{$y.Label}}</label>
      {{- end}}
    </fieldset>
    {{- end}}
    <button type="button" id="theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false">Dark mode</button>
  </div>
</header>

<main>

{{- /* THE HERO IS ABOVE THE CHART AND THE REST OF THE TILE ROW IS NOT. One
       number and one diagram is what this page is for; the six figures behind
       it open from "The figures behind it" below, and #figures moved with them.

       BOTH CONTAINERS ARE PAINTED, and that is why there are two ids rather
       than one section split by CSS. paintYearWords owns each wholesale, so a
       per-year figure rendered anywhere it does not write goes stale on the
       first year switch -- the fisc-kwq / fisc-yi4 / fisc-iyt defect. */}}
<section class="figures" id="hero" aria-label="What the city spends">
  <div class="tile hero">
    <div class="label">{{.Hero.Label}}</div>
    <div class="value">{{.Hero.Value}}</div>
    <div class="note">{{.Hero.Note}}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<h2>The flow</h2>

<div class="card">
  <div class="controls">
    <div class="legend" id="legend" aria-label="Fund groups"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="legend" aria-label="How to read the marks">
    <span><span class="key published" aria-hidden="true"></span> printed by the city</span>
    <span><span class="key derived" aria-hidden="true"></span> ◇ our inference (dashed)</span>
  </div>
  {{- /* SHIPPED EMPTY AND hidden, filled by app.js, on a page that declares
         no steps as much as on one that does. chart.html.tmpl says why it
         cannot be rendered server-side; unconditional here because the spine
         is the page a chain is declared on and the element has to be there
         before the declaration is. Hidden, it is not a control that lies. */}}
  <nav class="breadcrumb" id="breadcrumb" aria-label="Chart depth" hidden></nav>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <!-- role="group", not "img": an img is a leaf, and the marks inside carry
         tabindex and aria-label so a keyboard reader can walk the flows. Under
         role="img" they are reachable but announced as nothing, which the
         "tab to a flow" instruction below would then be lying about. -->
    <svg class="sankey" id="chart" role="group" aria-labelledby="chart-title chart-desc">
      {{- /* THE PACKAGER'S ONE STRING, not a composition of this template's
             own. app.js repaints this element from the same yearView field on
             a year switch, so a second wording composed here is an accessible
             name that silently reverts on the first toggle -- announced only
             to the readers who cannot see the marks and check. */}}
      <title id="chart-title">{{.ChartTitle}}</title>
      {{- /* "OPENS FROM" AND NOT "IS LISTED BELOW". The flow table used to ship
             open and now ships inside a closed <details>, which takes it out of
             the accessibility tree until the reader expands it -- so a
             screen-reader user told the figures were "listed below" would go
             looking for a table that is not, from their position, there. */}}
      <desc id="chart-desc">
        Revenue categories flow into six fund groups, which flow into object categories,
        transfers out and fund balance. The same figures are in the flow table below, which
        opens from the "Every flow, as a table" heading.
      </desc>
    </svg>
    <div class="tooltip" id="tooltip" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
  </div>
  <div class="detail" id="detail" aria-live="polite">
    <p class="subtle">Select a flow or a node to pin its provenance here.</p>
  </div>
  <noscript>
    <p>
      This page draws its chart in the browser from
      <a href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a>. With JavaScript off, that file and the
      figures on this page are still the whole story: every amount, fact id and source page
      is in it. The panels below open without a script, but two of them are FILLED by one:
      "Every flow, as a table" will be empty and "What we inferred" will still say it is
      loading. The file has both.
    </p>
  </noscript>
</div>

{{- /* THE READING OF THE CHART SITS UNDER THE CHART, and it is deliberately
       NOT disclosed. "Counting them as spending is exactly the error the
       headline avoids" is this page's central argument, and it is the only
       place that argument is made above a disclosure now that the tile row
       has moved below one. */}}
<p class="lede">
  Revenue categories on the left, the city's six fund groups in the middle, what the money
  is spent on at the right. Transfers between funds and movements of accumulated fund
  balance are drawn as their own endpoints, because they are not money crossing the city's
  boundary — counting them as spending is exactly the error the headline avoids.
</p>
<p class="lede subtle">
  Hover, tap or tab to a flow for its amount, fact ids and sources.
  Click a node or a fund swatch to follow only that money; Escape puts the rest back.
</p>

<!-- THE APPARATUS IS DISCLOSED, NOT DELETED. Three of the four panels below
     were top-level <h2>s on an open page, so a reader met four caveats -- 254
     words between them, the longest 174 -- and a 58-row table before reaching
     the end of a page whose subject is the chart above. The fourth, the tile
     row, had no heading at all and sat ABOVE that chart.

     THE DISCLOSURE ITSELF NEEDS NO SCRIPT, which is why it is markup rather
     than JavaScript: a closed <details> keeps its content in the DOM, so what
     is server-rendered survives with JavaScript off and is there to be opened.

     "IN THE DOM" IS NOT "AVAILABLE". A closed <details> is collapsed for
     assistive technology as well as visually -- its content is out of the
     accessibility tree and out of in-page find -- until the reader opens it.
     That is the trade this section makes on purpose, and it is why the chart's
     <desc> above says the figures "open from" a heading rather than that they
     are listed below.

     THAT IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE DISCLOSURE, NOT ABOUT EVERY PANEL BEHIND IT. The
     caveat summaries below really are server-rendered. #derived-list ships a
     "Loading..." placeholder and #flow-table an empty <tbody>; both are filled
     by app.js, and the <noscript> above names those two panels so a reader who
     opens an empty one knows why.

     What is NOT here at all is the caveats' full text -- that moved to
     caveats.html, and this page shows one-line summaries linking to it.

     The <h2>s stay inside the <summary>, so the document outline a screen
     reader walks is unchanged. -->
<details class="apparatus" id="figures-view">
  <summary>
    <h2>The figures behind it</h2>
    <span class="apparatus-hint">the working, including the column total that counts
      transfers twice</span>
  </summary>
  {{- /* THE WRONG ANSWER IS STILL HERE, struck through, and it is first. A
         figure a reader will otherwise compute for themselves is worth
         printing beside its refutation; what it is not worth is a tile above
         the chart. It stays inside .Figures rather than moving to a field of
         its own so that a year switch repaints it with everything else --
         headline-naive-expenditure checks the arithmetic behind it, and
         data/sankey*.json carries the cents. */}}
  <section class="figures" id="figures" aria-label="The figures behind the headline">
    {{- range .Figures}}
    <div class="tile{{if .Kind}} {{.Kind}}{{end}}">
      <div class="label">{{.Label}}</div>
      <div class="value">{{.Value}}</div>
      <div class="note">{{.Note}}</div>
    </div>
    {{- end}}
  </section>
</details>

<details class="apparatus" id="derived-view">
  <summary>
    <h2>What we inferred</h2>
    <span class="apparatus-hint">the classifications that are ours, not the city's</span>
  </summary>
  <p class="lede">
    A figure the city printed and a classification we made are different things, so they are
    never drawn alike. Everything listed here is ours, with the reasoning and the row it came
    from.
  </p>
  <ul class="derived-list" id="derived-list">
    <li class="subtle">Loading from {{.DataPath}}…</li>
  </ul>
</details>

{{- /* THE COUNT IN THIS SUMMARY IS PER-YEAR and paintYearWords repaints it.
       FY2025-26 carries four caveats and FY2026-27 five -- the extra one is the
       internal-service figure the city's own book contradicts -- so a count
       rendered once would go stale the moment the reader switches year, which
       is the fisc-kwq / fisc-yi4 / fisc-iyt defect in a new string. */}}
<details class="apparatus" id="caveats-view">
  <summary>
    <h2>Caveats</h2>
    <span class="apparatus-hint"><span id="caveats-count">{{len .Caveats}}</span> reasons this
      chart will not reconcile row-for-row against the printed schedule</span>
  </summary>
  <p class="lede">
    Every one of these ships with the data rather than in a footnote, so a reader who
    downloads <a href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a> gets them too.
    {{- if .CaveatsPath}} The full text of each is on
    <a href="{{.CaveatsPath}}">the caveats page</a>, beside every other document's.{{end}}
  </p>
  {{- /* SUMMARIES, NOT TEXTS, and the link is what makes that honest rather
         than a truncation: the paragraph still exists, on a page of its own,
         and the anchor names THIS document's copy of it. A caveat id can carry
         different text in different documents, so a bare #<id> would land a
         FY2026-27 reader on FY2025-26's sentence.

         THE LINK IS CONDITIONAL ON THERE BEING A PAGE. A single-view export --
         Options.views()'s default, and writeGolden -- writes index.html and
         nothing else, so an unconditional href would ship a dead link and
         TestEveryAssetThePageAsksForWasWritten would say so. */}}
  <ul class="caveats" id="caveats">
    {{- range .Caveats}}
    <li>{{if .Href}}<a href="{{.Href}}">{{.Summary}}</a>{{else}}{{.Summary}}{{end}}</li>
    {{- end}}
  </ul>
</details>

{{- /* #counts-line MOVED INTO THE SUMMARY and did not change what it says.
       app.js reaches it by id and fold.mjs and year.mjs read its textContent,
       none of which cares where it sits; a reader deciding whether to open the
       table is exactly who the sentence was written for. */}}
<details class="apparatus" id="table-view">
  <summary>
    <h2>Every flow, as a table</h2>
    <span class="apparatus-hint">the chart's twin, same numbers —
      <span id="counts-line">{{.Links}} flows between {{.Nodes}} nodes, from {{.Facts}} facts</span></span>
  </summary>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table id="flow-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">From</th>
          <th scope="col">To</th>
          <th scope="col">Amount</th>
          <th scope="col">Kind</th>
          <th scope="col">Basis</th>
          <th scope="col">Fact ids</th>
          <th scope="col">Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</details>

</main>

<footer>
  <!-- "every year this page can show", not "this chart's": the list below is
       unioned across the view's year stems (fisc-yi4), so with two years
       published it names pages the year on screen may not cite. Saying which
       set it is beats leaving a reader to assume it is the narrower one. -->
  {{- /* THE SOURCE LIST IS DISCLOSED AND THE SENTENCE BELOW IT IS NOT. The
         list is two links per cited page and grows with the corpus; the
         sentence under it names the scope, the basis and the document on
         screen, and a reader must never have to open something to learn which
         fiscal year they are reading. */}}
  <details class="apparatus" id="sources-view">
    <summary>
      <h3>Sources for every year this page can show</h3>
      <span class="apparatus-hint">{{len .Sources}} document{{if ne (len .Sources) 1}}s{{end}},
        every page this view cites, and {{len .Projections}} data file{{if ne (len .Projections) 1}}s{{end}}</span>
    </summary>
    <ul>
      {{- range .Sources}}
      <li>
        {{if .PDFURL}}<a href="{{.PDFURL}}">{{.Title}}</a>{{else}}{{.Title}}{{end}}{{if .Publisher}}, {{.Publisher}}{{end}}
        {{- if .Pages}} —
        {{- range $i, $p := .Pages}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}
          <a href="{{$p.PDFURL}}">p{{$p.Number}} (PDF)</a> /
          <a href="{{$p.TextURL}}">extracted text</a>
        {{- end}}{{end}}
      </li>
      {{- end}}
    </ul>
    <p class="subtle">
      {{- /* THE FILE LIST FOLDS WITH THE SOURCE LIST because it is the same
             kind of thing: a set of links that grows with the corpus, useful
             to a reader who came for the data and furniture to one who did
             not. What stays outside is the sentence naming the scope, the
             basis and the file THIS page is drawn from. */}}
      Every data file the site publishes, each fetchable on its own:
      {{- range $i, $p := .Projections}}{{if $i}},{{end}} <a href="{{$p.Path}}">{{$p.Path}}</a>{{end}}.
    </p>
  </details>
  <p class="subtle">
    Scope {{.Scope}}, basis <span id="page-basis">{{.Basis}}</span>. Projection: {{.ProjectionBy}}. Packaged by {{.ExportedBy}}.
    <!-- The two sentences are separate because the claims are. The scope and
         basis here are THIS PAGE'S, read out of the document it is drawn from;
         the source list and the file list, both inside the disclosure that
         closes above this paragraph, are every document and every file the SITE
         ships, and since it gained a second schedule they are no longer the
         same set. Running them together would say the revenue trends are
         all-funds-gross adopted FY2025-26, which is four wrong things about one
         file.

         THE BASIS IS WRAPPED AND THE SCOPE IS NOT, and the asymmetry is the
         point rather than an oversight. Basis is per-year -- yearView carries
         it, and paintYearWords rewrites this span on every switch, or the
         sentence would go on describing the year the reader left. Scope cannot
         vary across this view's years (Sankey.Slices fixes it), buildSankeyPage
         refuses a stem that disagrees, and the lede above says "all funds,
         gross" in prose that is not repainted either -- so a span here would
         repaint one of two copies of one claim. -->
    <span data-year-path>This chart is drawn from <a href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a>.</span>
  </p>
</footer>

<script src="vendor/d3.min.js"></script>
<script src="vendor/d3-sankey.min.js"></script>
<script>window.FISC_CONFIG = {{.ConfigJSON}};</script>
<script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
